Psoriasis
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Well-demarcated erythematous plaques, Silvery-white scale, Auspitz sign

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-033
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe generalized skin pain, massive scaling, and cracking of plaques resulting in significant bleeding and immobility.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a known history of psoriasis experiencing a sudden, explosive flare across the entire body. The severe skin tension and cracking of thick plaques caused significant pain, bleeding upon movement, and temporary immobility. Urgent out-of-hours assessment was required to exclude erythrodermic psoriasis or secondary staphylococcal sepsis, and to initiate potent systemic and topical therapies to rapidly restore skin barrier function and relieve severe mechanical pain.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is so tight and thick it feels like it's going to tear open if I move. My elbows and knees are bleeding continuously, and the pain is unbearable."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 10-year history of plaque psoriasis. Following a severe period of psychosocial stress and a recent streptococcal throat infection, they have experienced a massive, explosive flare over the past 7 days. The patient presents with widespread, well-demarcated erythematous plaques heavily covered with thick silvery-white scale. The predominant extensor distribution primarily involves the knees and elbows, but has spread extensively to the trunk. The patient notes severe localized skin soreness and profound tightness. Movement causes the plaques to fissure, resulting in painful bleeding. The patient's spouse notes a positive Koebner phenomenon, with new plaques developing at the site of a recent scratch on the forearm.
Functional Status: Temporarily bed-bound due to severe mechanical pain when moving joints covered by thick plaques.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe pain, immobility, and risk of secondary infection through fissured skin.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, lying rigidly in bed to avoid bending the knees or elbows, shedding copious amounts of silver scale onto the bedding.
Physical Exam: The bilateral extensor elbows, knees, lower back, and scalp exhibit massive, confluent, well-demarcated erythematous plaques. These are surmounted by dense, adherent silvery-white scale. Gentle removal of a scale fragment elicits the Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding from the underlying dilated capillaries). Deep, painful fissures are present across the antecubital and popliteal flexures where the plaques have cracked under tension. Nail pitting is prominent. Despite the widespread involvement, the patient is not erythrodermic (erythema covers <90% body surface area) and lacks high fever or rigors, ruling out acute generalized pustular psoriasis or sepsis.
Environmental Observations: Significant accumulation of shed skin scales on the floor and bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Non-compliant with prescribed topical calcipotriol/betamethasone due to the vast surface area involved.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Plaque Psoriasis - Status: Acute, explosive exacerbation
Differentials:

Erythrodermic Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of generalized body erythema >90%, stable vital signs, and absence of systemic toxicity or hypothermia.

Eczema Herpeticum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of monomorphic grouped vesicles or punched-out erosions, and lack of systemic fever.

Seborrheic Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of classic thick, adherent silvery scale (rather than greasy yellow scale), the Auspitz sign, and the predominant extensor/truncal distribution rather than purely facial/scalp.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to temporary immobility)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, acute flare of plaque psoriasis resulting in generalized skin pain, fissuring, and functional immobility due to the mechanical stiffness of the dense plaques. Triage appropriately flagged the generalized skin pain and bleeding as a high-acuity crisis to rule out life-threatening erythrodermic or pustular variants. Clinical examination confirms a severe classic plaque flare, heavily exacerbated by a recent strep infection (often triggering a guttate/plaque overlap). Immediate intervention to prescribe potent topical corticosteroids, massive amounts of emollients, and appropriate analgesia was critical to quickly soften the rigid plaques, stop the painful fissuring, and safely defer the patient to specialized dermatological care for systemic immunomodulators.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment BD for the most severely inflamed and fissured plaques. Advised massive, liberal application of a thick, greasy emollient (e.g., 50% white soft paraffin/50% liquid paraffin) QID to the entire body to rapidly soften the scale and restore flexibility. Administered Paracetamol 1000mg/Ibuprofen 400mg for skin pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: By immediately applying the thick, greasy ointment continuously throughout the day, you will instantly lock moisture into the rigid plaques, making them soft and flexible again so you can bend your arms and legs without agonizing cracking. By completely avoiding picking or peeling the thick scales, you will prevent the deep bleeding (Auspitz sign) and stop triggering new psoriasis patches from forming in the damaged skin.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Generated an urgent referral to a Dermatologist for consideration of systemic biologics or phototherapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3-5 days to monitor the flare, check for secondary infection, and ensure the dermatology referral is actioned.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If your skin suddenly becomes bright red all over your entire body, if you develop a high fever and uncontrollable shivering, or if the cracked skin begins leaking thick yellow pus, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Psoriasis is a systemic autoimmune condition. Topical creams are insufficient for a flare of this magnitude; systemic medications will likely be required from a specialist.
Clinical Course: The thick, greasy emollients will provide significant relief from the tightness and cracking within 24-48 hours. The redness and plaque thickness will require weeks to respond fully to treatment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your body temperature. Widespread skin inflammation can disrupt your ability to regulate heat. Seek emergency care if you feel freezing cold despite warm blankets.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent throat infection triggered a massive immune response, causing your skin cells to multiply 10 times faster than normal, piling up into these thick, rigid plaques. The steroid ointment will turn off the local inflammation, but massive moisturizing is the only way to stop the painful cracking.
Actionable Steps: Apply the thick emollient immediately after showering while the skin is damp. Wear loose, soft cotton clothing to minimize friction on the sensitive plaques.

